        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Sorry, I should have been more precise. I was not getting any snippets on files with a `.tsx` extension. `lang` is set to `tsx`. I incorrectly assumed `.jsx` files would set `lang` to `jsx` as well. I can provide reproduction steps if they are still needed.\n\nAh, I see. The core issue here is that 'mini.snippets' prefers language name of the locally active tree-sitter parser, which in this case is \"tsx\". You can interactively see the currently active default context (which includes language name) via `:=MiniSnippets.default_prepare({})` (the second table is the context). [It is documented](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/a84b7e555fe382c1859e6ca46b4983c822a1f77f/doc/mini-snippets.txt#L928) but probably mentioning it in `gen_loader.from_lang()` would be useful.\n\nLoading from 'package.json' of 'rafamadriz/friendly-snippets' would not have helped here as it doesn't define \"tsx\" as language, only [`typescriptreact`](https://github.com/rafamadriz/friendly-snippets/blob/efff286dd74c22f731cdec26a70b46e5b203c619/package.json#L257). So it wouldn't have helped in this particular case, which somewhat reinforces my belief to not go that route.\n\nHere is the setup that would work here:\n\n```lua\nlocal snippets = require('mini.snippets')\nlocal lang_patterns = { tsx = { '**/typescript.json', '**/react-ts.json', '**/next-ts.json' } }\n\nsnippets.setup({\n  snippets = {\n    snippets.gen_loader.from_file('~/.config/nvim/snippets/global.json'),\n    snippets.gen_loader.from_lang({ lang_patterns = lang_patterns }),\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAt the moment I also think that this type of explicit opt-in for the cases is a good thing for when snippet files are not obviously grouped together. This gives more control to the user without loading too much from the snippet \"provider\". Together with the mentioned language misalignment (which is even more severe if 'friendly-snippets' doesn't fully follow upstream specification), I think it makes a pretty good case of not having this kind of loader built-in.",
